  "cards": [
    {"dot": "cyan", "title": "Packets and conversations answer different questions", "items": ["Packet decoders inspect individual layers. TCP DNS/SIP framing belongs to the stream producer to avoid duplicate messages (gopacket_decoder.go:220-223).", "Conversation decoding uses server-port and byte evidence to select a protocol; UDP tracking is reached through the same enabled reassembly entry (tcp_connection.go:735-898).", "The normalizer preserves original evidence alongside logical packet views. Workers preserve symmetric-flow affinity (collector.go:830-913)."]},
    {"dot": "emerald", "title": "Context makes records useful", "items": ["Packet context carries Community ID for cross-tool correlation (internal/collector/worker.go:143-168).", "Host profiles combine observed peer/port activity with DNS, geolocation, DHCP and TLS JA4; native DPI work runs only when enabled (host.go:108-279).", "The output boxes are logical record families. The default sink is per-type Protobuf, with selectable CSV, JSON, Elasticsearch, channel and socket alternatives (internal/netio/writer.go:45-67)."]},
    {"dot": "amber", "title": "Coverage is conditional", "items": ["-include / -exclude select active decoders (gopacket_decoder.go:105-115). A configured feature is not proof that this capture supplied decodable content.", "No plaintext application claim follows from encrypted traffic alone. File extraction and explicit TLS-decryption workflows have their own conditions (docs/file-extraction.md:3; docs/mcp.md:84).", "This view expands decoding, not distributed transport or inline firewall enforcement. Start with overview.html for the complete capture-to-investigation journey."]}
  ]
